<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip II of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 359–<a href="/years/336-bce">336 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argead-dynasty">Argead dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> from 359 to 336 BCE and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. He professionalised the Macedonian army, introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and used diplomacy and conquest to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>. His reforms made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> possible, and he was assassinated in 336 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip II of Macedon" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel from Riyadh, Saudi Arabia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phillip_II,_king_of_Macedonia,_Roman_copy_of_Greek_original,_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen_(36420294055).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip was the youngest son of King Amyntas III and Eurydice of Lynkestis, born in 383 or 382 BCE. After the assassination of his brother Alexander II he was sent as a hostage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a>, and was later held at Thebes, then the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, from about 368 to 365 BCE, where he received a military and diplomatic education from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 364 BCE. When his brother Perdiccas III died in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> in 359 BCE, Philip was appointed regent for the infant Amyntas IV but took the kingdom for himself. He faced Paeonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> who had invaded the east and an Athenian force landed with a pretender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Philip drove back the Paeonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>, defeated 3,000 Athenian hoplites and strengthened his army. Its cavalry grew from 600 to 4,000 men by 334 BCE, soldiers could be promoted and rewarded, and he created the Macedonian phalanx. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> in the Erigon Valley, besieged Amphipolis in 357 BCE and kept it, with Pydna, after promising them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He founded Philippi at Crenides, whose mines gave him much of his gold. At the siege of Methone he lost his right eye. He took Olynthus in 348 BCE and razed it.</p><p>As a leader of the Thessalian League, Philip entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a> and won at the Battle of Crocus Field. He made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 346 BCE. His sieges of Perinthus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> failed, but at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE he defeated Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and was recognised as military leader of the League of Corinth, a Greek confederation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. In 336 BCE he sent Parmenion and 10,000 men into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to prepare an invasion to free the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the western coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philip was assassinated in October 336 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a>, while entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> during celebrations of the marriage of his daughter Cleopatra to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander I of Epirus</a>. One of his bodyguards, Pausanias of Orestis, stabbed him. The motives are disputed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> says only that Pausanias had been offended by Attalus. Later writers link the killing with Alexander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, but some modern historians find these accounts improbable. Alexander succeeded him.</p><p>Philip had seven wives. In 1977 Manolis Andronikos excavated the Great Tumulus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aigai">Aigai</a> near Vergina, and Tomb II is now generally held to be Philip's, partly because a skull shows damage to the right eye. He founded cities including Philippi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a>, today Plovdiv. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> honoured him with cult, such as an altar to Zeus Philippeios at Eresos and the Philippeion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip II of Macedon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens, urged on by Demosthenes, joined Thebes against <b>Philip II of Macedon</b> and was defeated at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The young <b>Philip II of Macedon</b> spent several years as a hostage at Thebes in the 360s BCE, at the height of Theban power, and probably learned from its generals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The city's most celebrated feat was its resistance to <b>Philip II of Macedon</b>, who besieged it in 340–339 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip II of Macedon</b> defeated Onomarchus at the Battle of the Crocus Field in 352 BCE and imposed a settlement in 346 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotys I fought Athens for the Thracian Chersonese in the 360s BCE, but divided Thracian forces could not withstand the reformed army of <b>Philip II of Macedon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Philip II of Macedon</b> had been raised in Thebes as a hostage and had learnt much of the art of war from Pelopidas, so the city also shaped the power that overthrew it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Aigai, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,22.32&amp;year=-348" aria-label="Aigai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5098 717 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Philip II of Macedon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 987h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,22.32&amp;year=-348">What was here in 348 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O my son, look thee out a kingdom equal to and worthy of thyself, for Macedonia is too little for thee.&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Alexander &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; to his son after the boy tamed the horse Bucephalus; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
